 Linear Intersection Approximation (also known as Midpoint Intersection) estimates the intersection point of an isosurface with a grid edge by calculating the geometric midpoint of the edge, rather than performing linear interpolation based on scalar values. It differs from its parent, [Linear Edge Intersection](868-linear-edge-intersection.md), by ignoring endpoint scalar values entirely, and from its sibling, [Iterative Bisection Intersection Refinement](880-iterative-bisection-intersection-refinement.md), by using a single fixed geometric step instead of recursive refinement [G].
 
+### [Triangle Point Generation (Linear Interpolation with Snap)](3506-triangle-point-generation-linear-interpolation-with-snap.md)
+This method computes the precise intersection point on a triangle edge where a scalar field equals a threshold, using linear interpolation between two grid vertices. It distinguishes itself from siblings like [Iterative Bisection Intersection Refinement](880-iterative-bisection-intersection-refinement.md) by avoiding iterative convergence, instead applying a deterministic "snap" heuristic to vertices near the threshold to stabilize the interpolation result. It serves as the geometric foundation for [Triangle Point Sampling](17806-triangle-point-sampling.md), which requires robust point generation to ensure uniform spatial distribution.
