 ### [Inverse Linear Interpolation](12154-inverse-linear-interpolation.md)
-Inverse Linear Interpolation calculates the non-integer position $x$ corresponding to a specific scalar value $v$ between two known values $v_1$ and $v_2$ at positions $x_1$ and $x_2$, assuming linear variation. It is primarily used for finding iso-surface intersections or specific density levels within a volume. Unlike [Linear Edge Intersection](868-linear-edge-intersection.md), which computes the exact intersection on a grid edge, this method provides the general algebraic inversion of linear interpolation.
+Inverse Linear Interpolation calculates the non-integer position $x$ corresponding to a specific scalar value $v$ between two known values $v_1$ and $v_2$ at positions $x_1$ and $x_2$. It solves the inverse interpolation problem for isosurface extraction by determining the exact location along a grid line segment where an interpolated scalar field takes on a specific isosurface value $T_m$. Unlike forward linear interpolation which estimates a value at a known position, this method inverts the relationship to find the exact position for a known value, which is necessary for precise isosurface mesh generation. It builds on [Linear Edge Intersection](868-linear-edge-intersection.md) by providing the specific arithmetic to resolve the intersection point analytically.
 
